    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d101">

                <pb facs="0189" n="85" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg85"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3661: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul at Geneva
                        (Gilbert)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-01-28T12:00:00-05:00">January 28,
                        1932—noon</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>30. Your 38, January 26, 9 p.m.<anchor n="63" xml:id="d101-f1"/> Inform Cecil as
                    strictly confidential to himself that, before receiving the information
                    contained in <pb facs="0190" n="86" xml:id="pg86"/> paragraph 5 of your telegram
                    under reference, your Government had instructed the American Ambassador,
                        Tokyo,<anchor n="64" xml:id="d101-f2"/> to urge upon the Japanese Foreign
                    Office the importance of the situation at Shanghai in relation to international
                    interests and world peace. Also, that the Secretary of State has noted with
                    interest and appreciation the position which Cecil has taken, as reported in
                    your paragraph 4, in support of the position of the United States in regard to
                    the Manchuria question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
